---
/**
 * @component Hero
 * @version   3.0.0
 * @updated   2026-09-11
 * @source    astro-template@0ad4fd0
 * @copied    2026-09-10
 * @modified  true
 *
 * The opening screen: a cut-out figure standing in the name.
 *
 * The composition is a single idea — the name set as large as the measure
 * allows, and a cut-out figure standing beside and slightly over it, so the two
 * read as one image rather than as a picture next to a heading. Everything else
 * (the mono strip, the numbered roles, the handwritten opening line) is small,
 * which is what leaves the page to the name and the photograph.
 *
 * WHY THE FIGURE IS BESIDE THE NAME AND NOT CENTRED ON IT. The reference for
 * this hero centres the figure over the name, and it works there because the
 * name is ghosted to a pale tint — so the face, being darker, wins. Multiply
 * keeps the darker layer, and that cuts both ways: with the name in ink it is
 * darker than a face, so "WILLIAM" prints straight across his eyes. The only
 * way to centre it is to drop the name to a tint pale enough for skin to beat,
 * which lands far under 3:1 — and this is the page's `h1`. So the name keeps
 * its contrast and the figure moves off to the side, overlapping only where it
 * is the dark shirt doing the occluding.
 *
 * On a phone there is no room to put anything beside anything, so the figure
 * goes above the name and the name is pulled back up into the bottom of it.
 * That is the same rule, applied vertically: the overlap lands on the chest,
 * well below the collar, and the face is never underneath type.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HOW THE CUT-OUT WORKS — read this before touching the portrait or the layout.
 *
 * There is no masking, no transparent PNG and no background removal. The
 * portrait is a studio headshot on a white backdrop, composited with
 * `mix-blend-mode: multiply`.
 *
 * `portrait-bw.jpg` did NOT arrive with a white backdrop, and this is the part
 * to repeat when the high-resolution version lands. The delivered file had a
 * smooth vertical lighting gradient behind the subject, running from 231 at the
 * top of the frame to 251 at the bottom — invisible as a photograph, fatal as a
 * multiply source, because anything below 255 multiplies into a visibly darker
 * rectangle on the page. Thresholding it out was not an option either: the
 * light polo shirt sits at 208-235, straight through the backdrop's own range,
 * so no cut-off separates them.
 *
 * What fixed it was a flat-field correction, which is exact here because the
 * gradient is purely vertical and the subject never reaches either side of the
 * frame. For each row, take the median of the ten leftmost and ten rightmost
 * pixels as that row's background level, smooth it over a few rows to keep JPEG
 * noise from banding, then scale the whole row so that level becomes 255. The
 * backdrop goes to 254-255 everywhere and the subject keeps its tones relative
 * to white — the chest band moved by about one level, from 228 to 229.
 *
 * If a replacement photo shows a grey box behind the figure, that is this, and
 * that is the fix.
 *
 * Multiply keeps the darker of the two layers. White over paper leaves paper,
 * so the backdrop disappears completely; the person is darker than the page
 * everywhere, so the person stays. The result is a true cut-out that costs one
 * CSS property, and it gets *better* with a black-and-white source, not worse.
 *
 * Four things this depends on, every one of which silently breaks it:
 *
 *   1. NO ANCESTOR OF THE IMAGE MAY FORM A STACKING CONTEXT. An element only
 *      blends with the backdrop inside the nearest isolating ancestor, so a
 *      `z-10`, an `opacity` below 1, a `transform`, a `filter` or a
 *      `will-change` anywhere up the tree leaves the figure blending against
 *      nothing and rendering as a plain white box. This is exactly what
 *      happened on the first attempt, with a `relative z-10` wrapper.
 *      It is also why the figure has no entrance animation: `rise-in` animates
 *      `transform`, which would isolate the image for the duration and flash
 *      the white box before popping.
 *      Layering here is therefore done with DOM order alone — h1, then the
 *      figure, then the corner text — and deliberately no `z-index` at all.
 *      Absolute positioning is fine; it only isolates once `z-index` is set.
 *   2. The page under it must stay light. On a dark surface multiply eats the
 *      figure instead of the backdrop.
 *   3. The portrait's backdrop must stay white. Not "light" — white. See the
 *      flat-field note above; a backdrop at 231 is already a visible box.
 *   4. `showPlaceholder={false}` is not optional. CustomPicture otherwise
 *      paints a blurred, 1.15x-scaled copy of the same photo behind the image;
 *      that copy multiplies too and rings the figure in grey.
 *
 * `grayscale` is applied here in CSS as well, and is now a genuine no-op:
 * `portrait-bw.jpg` measures a mean chroma spread of 0.0, i.e. it is already
 * true greyscale. It stays as a guard, because the obvious way to break the
 * look later is to drop a colour photograph in and not notice. (It sits on the
 * image itself, not on an ancestor — see point 1.)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ONE NUMBER TO KNOW: `FIGURE_WIDTH`.
 *
 * `src/assets/portrait-bw.jpg` is 400x400 and `CustomPicture` never upscales,
 * so 400px of real detail is all there is. The subject fills 97% x 96% of that
 * frame, so there is no margin to crop away and nothing to reclaim — the only
 * lever is how far the file gets stretched.
 *
 * It is set below to a size that stretches it about 1.25x, which a blended
 * black-and-white figure carries but a crisp framed colour photo would not.
 * That is why this hero is as large as it is and no larger.
 *
 * Note the phone and tablet sizes are DOWNscales of the same file, so they are
 * pin sharp; only the `lg` size stretches it.
 *
 * A ~1400px portrait on the same white backdrop lets this go to the full
 * reference proportions: raise the `lg` step of `FIGURE_WIDTH` and update
 * `sizes` to match. Nothing else in this file needs to change.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import type { ImageMetadata } from "astro";
import CustomPicture from "@ui/CustomPicture.astro";
import TextRender from "@ui/TextRender.astro";
import Button from "@ui/Button.astro";
import MetaLine from "@ui/MetaLine.astro";
import AmbientWash from "@ui/AmbientWash.astro";

export interface ImageItem {
  image: ImageMetadata | string;
  description?: string;
  subText?: string;
}

export interface HeroLink {
  label: string;
  link: string;
}

export interface Props {
  /** The name. This is the headline — not a job title. */
  title?: string;
  /** One sentence that sounds like a person wrote it. */
  intro?: string;
  /** The mono strip: "software · cloud · homelab · maken · fotografie". */
  meta?: string[];
  /** The numbered list beside the figure. Three. */
  roles?: string[];
  /** First is rendered solid, the rest outlined. Two is plenty. */
  ctas?: HeroLink[];
  imageItems?: ImageItem[];
}

const {
  title = "",
  intro = "",
  meta = [],
  roles = [],
  ctas = [],
  imageItems = [],
} = Astro.props;

const portrait = imageItems[0];

/** See the long note above before changing this. */
const FIGURE_WIDTH = "w-full sm:w-[22rem] lg:w-[33rem]";
/**
 * Pulls the figure back over the text column so the two overlap. It has to be
 * a margin rather than a transform: a transform on an ancestor of the blended
 * image forms a stacking context and the cut-out reverts to a white box.
 */
const FIGURE_OVERLAP = "lg:-ml-16";

/**
 * The name, split into the given name and everything after it.
 *
 * Set on two lines rather than left to wrap: at this size the column would
 * break "William van der Vegt" wherever it happened to run out, and a name is
 * not a paragraph. The split is on the first space, so any given name plus any
 * remainder works and a single-word name degrades to one line.
 *
 * The two halves are then set as a deliberate pair rather than as one long
 * headline — see the markup. The given name is the loud one; the surname is
 * quieter, lighter and in its own case, which is also why the tussenvoegsels
 * are left alone: "van der Vegt" is how the name is actually written, and
 * shouting it in capitals throws away that detail.
 */
const space = title.indexOf(" ");
const given = space > -1 ? title.slice(0, space) : title;
const family = space > -1 ? title.slice(space + 1) : "";
---

<section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-hairline bg-background">
  {/* The washes. A SIBLING painted before the content, never an ancestor of
      it — every layer in here carries a `blur()`, and a filter on an ancestor
      of the portrait would form a stacking context and collapse the cut-out
      into a white box. See AmbientWash's header note.

      The graph paper that used to sit in this corner is gone: the washes do
      the atmosphere here now, and the ruled pad reads as far more purposeful
      where it was kept, behind the homelab parts table. */}
  <AmbientWash intensity="strong" />

  <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
    {/* --- Top strip: what he does, and one sentence on it ---------------- */}
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 pt-10 sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between sm:gap-10">
      <div class="motion-safe:animate-rise-in">
        <MetaLine items={meta} shape="dots" size="sm" />
      </div>

      <div class="flex max-w-sm flex-col gap-5 sm:max-w-sm sm:items-end">
        {/* The opening line, in the handwriting face — the one sentence on the
            page that should read as written rather than set.

            It runs larger than the body copy because it has to: Caveat's
            x-height is small, and at body size a script face stops being
            informal and starts being hard to read. `leading-snug` pulls the
            lines back in, since the larger size would otherwise open the block
            up more than a note should be. */}
        {
          intro && (
            <p
              class="text-pretty font-hand text-[1.4rem] leading-snug text-blackText motion-safe:animate-rise-in sm:text-right sm:text-[1.55rem]"
              style="animation-delay: 90ms;"
            >
              <TextRender>{intro}</TextRender>
            </p>
          )
        }

      </div>
    </div>

    {/* --- Name and figure ------------------------------------------------
        Two columns, bottom-aligned, so the figure lands on the section's
        bottom edge and bleeds off it while the text column stops short. The
        figure carries a negative left margin so it encroaches on the name
        rather than sitting politely next to it.

        Note the complete absence of `z-index`: paint order is DOM order (text
        column first, figure second, so the figure wins), and adding one
        anywhere would isolate the figure and kill the blend. */}
    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col lg:mt-4 lg:flex-row lg:items-end lg:gap-4">
      {/* Below `lg` the name is pulled up INTO the bottom of the portrait. Set
          flush underneath it the name read as a caption hanging off the photo
          rather than as part of it; the two have to touch for the hero to be
          one composition, which is exactly what the `lg:-ml-16` does on
          desktop. There is no column gap on a phone for the same reason — the
          negative margin is the whole spacing decision, stated once.

          How far up is a real constraint, not taste. It must clear the face:
          multiply keeps the darker layer, so ink type wins over skin and
          "WILLIAM" would print straight across his eyes (see the note at the
          top of this file). Crossing the SHIRT is fine and is the intent — the
          shirt is light, so the name stays near-black on near-white and reads
          as a printed overlay. These values put the cap line on the chest,
          below the collar, at every width from 320 up.

          Margins are safe here where a transform would not be; see point 1 of
          the header note. Paint order is on our side too: `order-first` moves
          the figure in the flex order, and flex items paint in order-modified
          order, so the name is drawn over the figure on a phone and under it
          at `lg`. Both are what the layout wants, and neither needs a
          `z-index` — which would break the cut-out. */}
      <div class="-mt-11 min-w-0 pb-12 sm:-mt-14 lg:mt-0 lg:flex-1">
        {/* Deliberately wider than the column it sits in, so the longest line
            runs on under the figure rather than stopping short of it. The
            section's `overflow-hidden` is what keeps that from widening the
            page. */}
        <h1
          class="motion-safe:animate-rise-in lg:w-[42rem]"
          style="animation-delay: 40ms;"
        >
          {/* The given name: the loud half. Capitals, heavy, tracked in tight
              enough that the letters nearly touch. */}
          <span class="block text-[clamp(3rem,14vw,7.5rem)] font-semibold uppercase leading-[0.82] tracking-[-0.04em] text-primaryText">
            <TextRender>{given}</TextRender>
          </span>

          {/* The surname: the quiet half, and deliberately a different voice
              rather than the same one turned down. Three things change at once
              — weight (semibold to light), case (capitals to the name as it is
              actually written), and colour (ink to muted) — because changing
              only one reads as an accident, and changing all three reads as a
              pair.

              It keeps roughly half the given name's size, which is small enough
              to be clearly subordinate and large enough to still hold the
              bottom of the block. The tracking goes the other way too: the
              given name is tightened to -0.04em, this is opened slightly, so
              the two halves sit differently on the line rather than looking
              like one headline in two weights. */}
          {
            family && (
              <span class="mt-2 block text-[clamp(1.75rem,7.2vw,3.9rem)] font-light leading-[1] tracking-[0.01em] text-mutedText sm:mt-3">
                <TextRender>{family}</TextRender>
              </span>
            )
          }
        </h1>

        {
          roles.length > 0 && (
            <ol class="mt-9 flex flex-col gap-1.5">
              {roles.map((role, i) => (
                <li
                  class="flex items-baseline gap-3 motion-safe:animate-rise-in"
                  style={`animation-delay: ${240 + i * 80}ms`}
                >
                  {/* The index is furniture, so it is hidden from assistive
                      tech — an ordered list already announces position, and
                      "zero one" read before each role is noise. */}
                  <span
                    class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-mutedText"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                  >
                    ({String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")})
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-xl font-medium italic tracking-tight text-primaryText sm:text-2xl">
                    <TextRender>{role}</TextRender>
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ol>
          )
        }

        {/* Stacked and full width on a phone, inline from `sm` up. Left to
            wrap, the two buttons sat at their own content widths in a ragged
            column; equal blocks read as a deliberate pair and give a thumb
            something to aim at. */}
        {
          ctas.length > 0 && (
            <div
              class="mt-9 flex flex-col gap-3 motion-safe:animate-rise-in sm:flex-row sm:flex-wrap"
              style="animation-delay: 360ms;"
            >
              {ctas.map((cta, i) => (
                <Button
                  href={cta.link}
                  variant={i === 0 ? "primary" : "secondary"}
                  arrow={i === 0}
                  class="justify-center sm:justify-start"
                >
                  <TextRender>{cta.label}</TextRender>
                </Button>
              ))}
            </div>
          )
        }
      </div>

      {/* `order-first` below `lg`: in source order the figure follows the text
          column, which put it after the buttons on a phone — the headshot
          arrived last, as an afterthought, when it is supposed to be the thing
          you land on. `order` is safe here where a transform would not be: it
          does not form a stacking context, so the multiply cut-out survives
          it. */}
      {
        portrait?.image && (
          <div class:list={["order-first shrink-0 lg:order-none lg:mx-0", FIGURE_WIDTH, FIGURE_OVERLAP]}>
            {/* Quality is high on purpose: the file is a few hundred pixels
                square, so this costs single-digit KB, and it is the only
                photograph of a face above the fold. `showPlaceholder` must
                stay false and the filter must stay on the image — see the
                header note. */}
            {/* The bottom fade is mobile-only, and it earns its place.
                The source frame cuts the shoulders off square. On desktop that
                never shows, because the figure bleeds past the section's bottom
                edge — but with the figure moved up the page on a phone, the cut
                lands mid-scroll as a hard horizontal slice across his chest.
                Dissolving the last quarter into the page hides an arbitrary
                crop and suits a cut-out better than a straight edge does.

                The mask sits on the SAME element as the blend, which is why it
                is safe: a mask does form a stacking context, but the blending
                element's own stacking context is the thing being composited,
                not a boundary. Only an ANCESTOR with one breaks the cut-out.
                Verified at 390 / 768 / 1440. */}
            <CustomPicture
              alt={portrait.description}
              classes="aspect-square w-full grayscale mix-blend-multiply [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_bottom,black_74%,transparent_100%)] lg:[mask-image:none]"
              sizes="(max-width: 639px) 100vw, (max-width: 1023px) 352px, 528px"
              srcImagePath={portrait.image}
              priority={true}
              qualityOverride={86}
              showPlaceholder={false}
            />
          </div>
        )
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
